          "description": "Parthenitae. Colony comprised of active rediae, densely concentrated in snail gonad with dispersion into basal visceral mass regions. Rediae translucent orange to colorless; ~ 750 – 2000 µm long, oblong to elongate (length: width up to ~ 5: 1), with posterior appendages that are often not pronounced. Cercaria. Body opaque white; non-oculate; with oral and ventral sucker; with main excretory ducts forming a broad “ v ”; with basal portions of main excretory ducts being particularly inflated and filled with large, distinct granules; collar with 25 collar spines in a single row, and 4 in a second row (2 pairs of “ corner spines ”); body ~ 550 µm long, ~ equal in length to tail; tail dorso-ventrally finned. Cercaria behavior: Fresh, emerged cercariae remain in water column, swim ~ continuously, body ventrally flexed (~ forming an overall spheroidal shape), lashing tail back and forth. Similar species: Hisb is most readily distinguished from all the echinostomatoids by having a tail fin.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:2505708"
